\description{
Read an ANDI-MS netCDF chromatogram, estimate a smoothly time-varying point
spread function, recover log-concave compounds with \code{\link[logconNMF:logconNMF]{logconNMF::logconNMF()}}, and
write a NIST-compatible MSP spectral library. All original mass channels
are retained in the final spectra.
}
\details{
\strong{File formats.} ANDI-MS variables \code{scan_index}, \code{point_count},
\code{scan_acquisition_time}, \code{mass_values} and \code{intensity_values}
define the scan spectra. Scan indices are zero-based. Padding between scans
is accepted. Intensities must be finite and nonnegative. Times are converted
to minutes; absent units use the ANDI-MS seconds convention. The selected
retention-time interval is read directly from the point arrays.

MSP entries are ordered by profile apex time, with a unique compound name,
retention time and integrated ion intensity in the comments, followed by
mass/intensity pairs. Exported spectra have base peak 999 and use
\code{ion_threshold}. \code{coef()} returns thresholded fitted ion totals;
\code{normalize="l1"} returns unit-L1 spectra. The original factors remain
available in \code{model$S}. The spreadsheet's compact spectrum text uses
the same cutoff, followed by display rounding.
Their compound profiles are \eqn{P_{ir}=H_{ir}\sum_j S_{rj}}, preserving
\eqn{HS=PT}. The workbook reports \code{area}, equal to \eqn{\sum_j S_{rj}}, in
intensity-scans, and \code{area_min}, the trapezoidal integral of
\eqn{P_{\cdot r}} over retention time, in intensity-minutes.

The returned object stores the factors and source CDF path. Observed data
are loaded from the CDF only when a plot needs them. Fitted signals are
reconstructed from the factors; the observed matrix is released after fitting.

\strong{PSF and background.} \code{\link[logconNMF:estimatePSF]{logconNMF::estimatePSF()}} estimates constant or smoothly
time-varying PSFs from the sample, optionally starting from an alkane standard.
Original channels, outcome groups and the total-ion signal are supported.
Interpolated columns are projected to log concavity on their local support
and normalized to unit sum. Their typical full width at half maximum,
\eqn{w}, sets the background dimension to
\deqn{q=\max\{4,\min[40,3+\lceil n/(50w)\rceil]\}.}
This places knots about 50 narrow-peak FWHMs apart. Each background
basis coefficient has a channel-specific spectrum, allowing slow spectral
drift across the chromatogram. Rank selection is conditional on this basis.
Background coefficients are nonnegative and unpenalized. This keeps the
baseline much smoother than an individual elution peak.
The calibrated median PSF width also sets the guard for the final
broad-profile fusion pass in \code{\link[logconNMF:logconNMF]{logconNMF::logconNMF()}}. Set
\code{control=list(profile_fusion=FALSE)} to disable that pass.

\strong{Shared noise model.} Noise parameters are estimated once from the
original channels and reused in PSF estimation and factorization. The PSF
weights include the information adjustment \eqn{1/\kappa}, where
\eqn{\kappa=1+2\widehat\rho_1}. Aggregation adjusts the variance parameters
according to the selected family; NB2 and ordinary Gamma use moment matching.
See \code{\link[logconNMF:estimatePSF]{logconNMF::estimatePSF()}} for the distributional details. The returned
\code{psf_working_weight} summarises the average Poisson-scale precision for
comparing noise scales; the actual PSF likelihood uses the specified family.

With the conditional count solver, a grouped PSF regression supplies starting
spectra. The final iterations use every ion, count the positive spectral
coefficients and the free ordinates of each log-concave curve. Multiple
samples share spectra on their native scan grids. Explicit numeric lambda
is per fitted dimension for this solver. See the mathematical basis article.

With the general solver, \code{precluster=32L} performs selection and fusion in at most 32 size-adjusted
outcome groups, followed by refinement on every original channel. The final
original-channel cleanup checks deletions, local fusions and missed residual
peaks using the complete compound-count criterion. Its diagnostics are in
\code{model$cleanup}; \code{control=list(original_cleanup=FALSE)} skips it. See
\code{\link[logconNMF:logconNMF]{logconNMF::logconNMF()}} for the selection criterion and shape constraints. The
backend uses a permissive component initialization, native log-concave
profile/spectrum refinement, and final rank selection with the requested
working-correlation adjustment. Small fragments contained in a broad peak
are assessed against equally refined unmerged local fits on every original
ion channel. Trial diagnostics are in \code{model$contained_fusion}; the
initialization criterion and history are in \code{model$initialization}.
Initial component selection uses the solver configured by
\code{control$initial_solver}, a named list of \code{\link[L0glm:controlSolverGen]{L0glm::controlSolverGen()}}
arguments. For a full chromatogram, for example,
\code{control=list(initial_solver=list(solver="eigen", method=3L, precond=1L))}
requests conjugate gradients with diagonal preconditioning. \code{max.memory}
in that list specifies the Gram/factor workspace budget in GiB. By default,
a Cholesky workspace-budget error triggers a reported retry with this CG
solver, preserving the memory limit and selection settings. Set
\code{control=list(initial_fallback=FALSE)} to disable the retry.
Compatible Poisson/quasipoisson fits reuse the ion groups to screen local
width restarts. Promising candidates and matched controls are refined and
accepted using all original channels, with background and noise settings
fixed. Other cases retain original-channel screening. Screening channel
counts, accepted changes and cache diagnostics are in \code{model$profile_starts}.
The
spreadsheet and returned coefficient rows use increasing apex retention time. Rank selection,
hard log concavity, censored Gamma intensities and compound-count information
criteria follow \code{\link[logconNMF:logconNMF]{logconNMF::logconNMF()}}.
The conditional solver uses grouped PSF regressions followed by original-ion
updates of shared spectra, sample profiles and background. Its peak memory
includes the raw count matrices and fitted means. For disk-backed joint
refinement use \code{batch=list(cache=FALSE)}.
}
\section{Experimental GC/MS examples}{

The \href{../articles/gcms-deconvolution.html}{GC/MS deconvolution tutorial}
converts the bundled \link[logconNMF:gcmsData1]{gcmsData1} and \link[logconNMF:gcmsData2]{gcmsData2} matrices to CDF, fits the
full-channel models and exports their spectra. It includes timings,
information criteria and all plot types, alongside the algorithm.
\if{html}{
\strong{gcmsData1: overlapping cuticular hydrocarbons.}
\figure{gcms-data1-traces.png}{Background-corrected observed mass traces and fitted compounds.}
\figure{gcms-data1-heatmaps.png}{Observed, corrected, fitted and Pearson-residual heatmaps.}
\figure{gcms-data1-profiles-spectra.png}{Recovered elution profiles and mass spectra.}
\strong{gcmsData2: beer volatiles with an asymmetric acid peak.}
\figure{gcms-data2-traces.png}{Observed ion traces and fitted beer-volatile components.}
\figure{gcms-data2-heatmaps.png}{Full-channel signal and Pearson-residual heatmaps.}
\figure{gcms-data2-profiles-spectra.png}{Representative compound profiles and mass spectra.}
}
}

\examples{
library(logconNMF)
\dontrun{
library(L0glm)
data("gcmsData1", package = "logconNMF")
data("gcmsData2", package = "logconNMF")
out <- file.path(tempdir(), "L0glm-GCMS")
dir.create(out, showWarnings = FALSE)
writeGCMSCDF(gcmsData1, file.path(out, "GCMS_data1.cdf"))
writeGCMSCDF(gcmsData2, file.path(out, "GCMS_data2.cdf"))

fit1 <- gcmsDeco(file.path(out, "GCMS_data1.cdf"),
  file.path(out, "GCMS_data1.msp"), nwindows = 1L, precluster = 7L,
  baseline_df = 1L,
  correlation = "auto", ncores = 8L,
  control = list(initial_relaxed = FALSE, fusion_passes = 0L),
  psf_control = list(kernel_est = convKernel("gaussian", 65L,
    list(sigma = 9)), maxiter = 0L))
fit2 <- gcmsDeco(file.path(out, "GCMS_data2.cdf"),
  file.path(out, "GCMS_data2.msp"), nwindows = 1L, precluster = 32L,
  correlation = "auto", ncores = 8L,
  control = list(initial_relaxed = TRUE, fusion_passes = 0L),
  psf_control = list(kernel_est = convKernel("gaussian", 65L,
    list(sigma = 6)), maxiter = 0L))
fit1$compounds
GIC(fit2)
coef(fit2, normalize = "l1")

plot(fit1, type = "lines", nrows = 1L,
  minutes_per_panel = diff(range(fit1$time)) + .01, labels = TRUE)
plot(fit1, type = "heatmap")
plot(fit1, type = "profiles", nrows = 3L)
plot(fit2, type = "lines", file = file.path(out, "GCMS_data2_traces.pdf"),
  nrows = 1L, minutes_per_panel = diff(range(fit2$time)) + .01)
plot(fit2, type = "heatmap", file = file.path(out, "GCMS_data2_heatmaps.pdf"))
plot(fit2, type = "profiles", file = file.path(out, "GCMS_data2_profiles_and_spectra.pdf"))

# For a full chromatogram with a separate calibration standard:
if (file.exists("beer.cdf") && file.exists("alkanes.cdf")) {
  beer <- gcmsDeco("beer.cdf", "beer.msp", alkanes_file = "alkanes.cdf",
    xlsx_file = "beer.xlsx", nwindows = 10L, precluster = 32L)
}

# Automatic PSF estimation from a bundled window and its named alkane TIC.
psf <- estimatePSF(gcmsData2, nwindows = 3L)
dim(psf$X)

# A CDF contains the chromatogram; pass the R calibration attribute explicitly.
calibrated <- gcmsDeco(file.path(out, "GCMS_data2.cdf"),
  file.path(out, "GCMS_data2_calibrated.msp"), nwindows = 3L, precluster = 32L,
  psf_control = list(standard = attr(gcmsData2, "alkanes_TIC")))
}

\dontrun{
# Sixteen related samples, retaining all mass channels. Compounds occur in
# half the samples on average, with retention drift and concentration-dependent
# widths and tails. Here the simulated quasipoisson dispersion is known.
library(logconNMF)
sim <- simulateGCMSSamples(samples=16L, n=1200L, k=48L,
  preset="time_area", presence=0.5, irregular=0, drift=2,
  family="quasipoisson", dispersion=2, seed=9118)
input <- file.path(tempdir(), "GCMSprime_samples16")
dir.create(input, showWarnings=FALSE)
for(s in sim$samples) {
  writeGCMSCDF(s$Y, file.path(input, paste0(s$sample_id, ".cdf")),
    time=s$time, acquired_at=s$acquired_at)
}
rm(sim, s)                         # observations are read from the CDF files
experiment <- gcmsDeco(input, output_dir=paste0(input, "_results"),
  family=quasipoisson("identity"), dispersion=2, correlation="none",
  nwindows=2L, baseline_df=4L, precluster=32L, ncores=16L,
  psf_control=list(maxiter=3L),
  batch=list(coupling=list(cycles=2L, initial_cycles=1L, rounds=1L, q=32L)))
experiment$seconds
experiment$parallel
experiment$samples[c("sample_name", "compounds", "GIC")]
experiment$areas
plot(experiment, type="areas")
plot(experiment, sample=1L, type="lines")
plot(experiment, sample=1L, type="profiles", which=1:6)
saveRDS(experiment$channel_groups, file.path(input, "channel_groups.rds"))
}
}
